  "articleBody": "Ever tripped over your own doorway? You're not alone! Thresholds can become real hazards if they're not quite right. Often, it comes down to the 'rise' – that's the measured height of the threshold. If it's over a half-inch, it can really catch your foot. Even smaller edges, if they're not properly beveled, can be a problem, especially if you're using a mobility device. Another common issue is door swing interference; sometimes the threshold isn't trimmed correctly, or the ramp chosen doesn't allow the door to open and close smoothly. It’s important to make sure there's enough clearance. Things like ignoring the weight capacity of a ramp or not checking surface conditions before installation can also turn a helpful solution into a new hazard. You really want to avoid guessing when it comes to these measurements. If you’re struggling with an uneven or difficult threshold, getting a professional to take a look can save you a lot of grief and potential injury. They can help make sure your entryway is safe and functional.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
